    "seo": {
      "title": "Insurance Comparison Platforms in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "How Iranian insurance comparison platforms present quotations, support purchases, and differ from the insurers providing coverage."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "comparison-and-distribution",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A shared interface for different insurers",
        "content": "Insurance comparison platforms make an otherwise fragmented purchasing process easier to navigate by presenting products from different providers in one place. Azki advertises comparison of insurance prices and services alongside online purchasing and installment options. Bimeh.com likewise presents quotation, comparison, and purchase as connected services. These are descriptions of their offered functions, rather than independently verified findings about customer outcomes. [s1][s2]\n\nThe distinction between a distribution platform and an insurer matters. The International Association of Insurance Supervisors, an international standard-setting body, treats insurance intermediation as an activity involving the solicitation, negotiation, or sale of insurance. Its framework distinguishes this activity from supplementary work such as claims administration and loss appraisal. A platform can therefore participate in several stages of the customer relationship without those functions becoming interchangeable. This international framework supplies terminology; it does not establish an individual Iranian platform’s authorization. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "quotation-inputs",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why quotations require personal and vehicle information",
        "content": "Bimeh.com’s motor third-party insurance interface illustrates how comparison depends on structured inputs. It offers an inquiry route using a vehicle registration plate and the plate owner’s national identification code, as well as a route without a plate. Its published explanation says that vehicle details and previous policy information feed the quotation process. This is a specific product workflow, not evidence that every insurance category uses identical questions. [s2]\n\nThe same page identifies vehicle characteristics, use, selected financial coverage, and claims-free history among the inputs relevant to price. Consequently, two displayed premiums are meaningful comparisons only when the underlying coverage and customer information are aligned. Installment availability introduces another distinction: the platform explains that participating insurers offering this option are identified within its quotation results. A payment arrangement should therefore be understood separately from the insurance protection being compared. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "limits-of-public-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What public descriptions cannot establish",
        "content": "Neither inspected purchasing interface establishes that its results constitute an exhaustive, independently ranked survey of the market. This research also did not inspect platform commission agreements or ranking algorithms. Claims about the cheapest offer, impartial ordering, or superior claims service would require additional evidence.\n\nThe useful editorial distinction is between making alternatives visible and proving their relative quality. The inspected pages support an account of comparison and purchasing functions. They do not support a league table of platforms, an estimate of their market shares, or a conclusion about which insurer consistently delivers better outcomes. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
